# Wireshark Networking & VoIP Terminology Guide

This guide explains the most important networking and VoIP terms that students commonly encounter while using Wireshark for packet analysis.

---

# 1. TCP (Transmission Control Protocol)

## Full Form

Transmission Control Protocol

## Purpose

Provides reliable communication between two devices.

## Features

- Reliable
- Connection-Oriented
- Error Checking
- Ordered Delivery

## Examples

- HTTP
- HTTPS
- SSH
- FTP

## Real-Life Example

Sending a registered courier:

```text
Sender → Courier → Receiver
Confirmation Received
```

TCP ensures data reaches the destination correctly.

---

# 2. UDP (User Datagram Protocol)

## Full Form

User Datagram Protocol

## Purpose

Provides fast communication with minimal overhead.

## Features

- Fast
- Connectionless
- No delivery guarantee
- No ordering guarantee

## Examples

- VoIP Calls
- Video Streaming
- Online Gaming
- DNS

## Real-Life Example

A live radio broadcast:

```text
Broadcast Starts
↓
Missed Part = Lost Forever
```

---

# TCP vs UDP

| Feature | TCP | UDP |
|----------|----------|----------|
| Reliable | Yes | No |
| Fast | No | Yes |
| Connection Required | Yes | No |
| Error Checking | Yes | Limited |
| Examples | HTTP, HTTPS, SSH | RTP, DNS, Streaming |

---

# 3. SYN (Synchronize)

## Purpose

Initiates a TCP connection.

## Meaning

```text
"Can we start communication?"
```

## Example

Client:

```text
SYN
```

---

# 4. ACK (Acknowledgement)

## Purpose

Confirms receipt of data.

## Meaning

```text
"I received your message."
```

## Example

Server:

```text
ACK
```

---

# 5. SYN-ACK

## Purpose

Acknowledges a SYN request and agrees to communicate.

## Meaning

```text
"I received your request and I'm ready."
```

---

# 6. FIN (Finish)

## Purpose

Gracefully terminates a TCP connection.

## Meaning

```text
"I have finished communication."
```

---

# 7. RST (Reset)

## Purpose

Immediately terminates a connection.

## Meaning

```text
"Connection aborted."
```

---

# TCP Three-Way Handshake

Before communication begins:

```text
Client
   |
   | SYN
   v
Server

Client
   <----------------
        SYN-ACK

Client
   ---------------->
          ACK

Connection Established
```

---

# 8. SIP (Session Initiation Protocol)

## Full Form

Session Initiation Protocol

## Purpose

Used to establish, manage, and terminate VoIP calls.

## Think of SIP as

```text
Call Manager
```

SIP handles:

- Calling
- Ringing
- Call Acceptance
- Call Termination

## Common Applications

- Linphone
- Zoiper
- Softphones
- IP Telephony

---

# SIP Call Flow

```text
Caller
   |
   | INVITE
   v

Receiver

100 Trying

180 Ringing

200 OK

ACK

Voice Communication Starts

BYE

Call Ends
```

---

# Common SIP Messages

## INVITE

Meaning:

```text
"I want to call you."
```

---

## 100 Trying

Meaning:

```text
"I received your request."
```

---

## 180 Ringing

Meaning:

```text
"The phone is ringing."
```

---

## 200 OK

Meaning:

```text
"The call has been accepted."
```

---

## ACK

Meaning:

```text
"I received your acceptance."
```

Voice communication begins.

---

## BYE

Meaning:

```text
"The call is over."
```

---

# 9. RTP (Real-time Transport Protocol)

## Full Form

Real-time Transport Protocol

## Purpose

Carries voice and video data during a VoIP call.

## Think of RTP as

```text
Voice Carrier
```

While SIP sets up the call:

```text
SIP → Creates Call
RTP → Carries Voice
```

---

# SIP vs RTP

| SIP | RTP |
|-------|-------|
| Establishes Call | Transfers Voice |
| Manages Session | Carries Audio |
| Handles Ringing | Handles Conversation |
| Controls Call | Transports Media |

---

# VoIP Communication Flow

```text
Linphone

SIP INVITE
      ↓

Call Accepted

      ↓

RTP Packets Start

      ↓

Voice Communication
```

---

# 10. DNS (Domain Name System)

## Full Form

Domain Name System

## Purpose

Converts domain names into IP addresses.

## Example

```text
google.com
      ↓
142.250.x.x
```

## Real-Life Example

DNS works like a phonebook:

```text
Name
 ↓
Phone Number
```

---

# 11. ICMP (Internet Control Message Protocol)

## Full Form

Internet Control Message Protocol

## Purpose

Used for diagnostics and network testing.

## Common Tool

```bash
ping
```

## Example

```text
Computer A
   ↓
"Are you alive?"

Computer B
   ↓
"Yes, I am alive."
```

---

# 12. HTTP (HyperText Transfer Protocol)

## Full Form

HyperText Transfer Protocol

## Purpose

Transfers web pages between browser and server.

## Default Port

```text
80
```

## Example

```text
Browser
    ↓
HTTP Request
    ↓
Web Server
    ↓
HTTP Response
```

---

# 13. HTTPS (HyperText Transfer Protocol Secure)

## Full Form

HyperText Transfer Protocol Secure

## Purpose

Encrypted web communication.

## Default Port

```text
443
```

## Difference from HTTP

| HTTP | HTTPS |
|--------|--------|
| Not Encrypted | Encrypted |
| Port 80 | Port 443 |
| Less Secure | More Secure |

---

# 14. SSH (Secure Shell)

## Full Form

Secure Shell

## Purpose

Secure remote login to Linux systems.

## Default Port

```text
22
```

## Example

```bash
ssh user@192.168.1.10
```

---

# 15. FTP (File Transfer Protocol)

## Full Form

File Transfer Protocol

## Purpose

Transfers files between systems.

## Default Port

```text
21
```

---

# 16. DHCP (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol)

## Full Form

Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol

## Purpose

Automatically assigns IP addresses.

## Example

```text
Connect Laptop
      ↓
DHCP Assigns IP
      ↓
Internet Access Available
```

---

# 17. ARP (Address Resolution Protocol)

## Full Form

Address Resolution Protocol

## Purpose

Converts IP Address into MAC Address.

## Example

```text
Known:
192.168.1.10

Need:
MAC Address

ARP Request
      ↓
ARP Reply
```

